# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DATA FREEZE, AND WHY PART OF IT IS NOT COMMITTED
#
# Every prior Cascadia module commits data/raw/ outright, because an EDGAR
# response is a few hundred kilobytes. This module's snapshot is a 314 MB zip
# that expands to 2.0 GB of tab-delimited text, and git is the wrong container
# for it.
#
# The freeze is therefore asserted by HASH, not by tracking the bytes:
# data/raw/cv88on.zip.sha256 and governance/source-register.md are committed
# and are the authoritative record of what was pulled and when.
# src/build_conformed.py re-computes that hash on every build and exits
# non-zero if it moved. This line used to name validate_freeze.py, which
# computes no hash at all -- it is the OTHER half of the gate, comparing the
# committed derived artifacts against a baseline with `git diff`.
#
# The snapshot below is deliberately NOT in that script's protected_paths.
# A gitignored path there FAILS OPEN: Path.glob matches the filesystem, so
# it resolves and is counted, and `git diff --quiet` then exits 0 because
# git reports no change on a file it does not track. It would read as
# protection and be none. See governance/freeze.toml.
#
# Everything DERIVED from the snapshot -- data/reference/ and data/conformed/
# -- is committed, and is small.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/raw/cv88on.zip
data/raw/*.txt
data/raw/*.part

# The conformed database is a derived artifact, rebuilt from the freeze
# by src/build_conformed.py. Large, and reproducible -- not committed.
data/conformed/*.duckdb
data/raw/cv88on.utf8.txt
